  "query_preview": "..Another day of showers and thunderstorms is in store \nfor today, with the main areas being along and south of the I-40 \ncorridor. Drier air is starting to filter into the region as the \nflow turns more to the west early this morning, then will continue \nas we see a more northwesterly flow develop. This will push the \nmoisture to the south through the day today. PWATs have already \nstarted to drop but still remain in the 75th-90th percentile for \nthis time of year. Any storms that develop today will be capable of \nheavy rainfall, gusty/erratic outflow winds, small hail, and \nlightning. Once the sun sets, we should see the activity begin \nto diminish across the forecast area. \n\nAs we head into tonight, the moisture continues to push to the \nsouth, though we don't fully lose it through the week. While much of \nthe area will dry out and heat up this week, there will remain \nchances for isolated showers, mainly across the eastern Mogollon Rim \nand White Mountains, each afternoon. \n\nWith the drier conditions and warming temperatures, we will again \nsee the lower elevations of the Grand Canyon reach between 105-110 \ndegrees each afternoon. If you have plans to be in the canyon, then \nmake sure you are prepared for hot conditions. \n\nA weak low tries to form around mid week, but it will mostly just \nbring some stronger winds to the region. These winds will arrive \naround Tuesday and continue through Wednesday, with elevated winds \ncontinue through the overnight areas downwind of the higher terrain. \n\nDrier conditions along with the expected stronger winds across the \nregion, will bring enhanced fire weather conditions to far northern \nand eastern Arizona for Tuesday through Friday. Just another thing \nto keep an eye on as we go through the week. \n\nBy the weekend, we see another couple of shortwaves approach the \narea, which should help to cool temperatures slightly.",
